No, you still need tools like LinkFinder and SecretFinder. Puppeteer does not replace them; it actually makes them significantly more powerful. [1, 2] 
Here is why you should combine them rather than choosing one over the other.
## The Limitation of Puppeteer Alone
Puppeteer only sees the JavaScript code that actually executes while you are clicking around a page. [3] 

* If a website has a massive main.js file containing 50 hidden API endpoints, but only 5 of those endpoints are used on the dashboard, Puppeteer will only capture those 5 active network requests.
* The other 45 endpoints remain completely hidden inside the dead JavaScript code.

## Where LinkFinder & SecretFinder Excel
Tools like LinkFinder and SecretFinder don't care about running the website. They are static analysis tools. They scan through the raw JavaScript source text line-by-line using regular expressions (Regex) to pull out every single URL, API route, AWS key, or hardcoded password hidden inside the code, even if that code never runs during a normal user session.
## The Ultimate Combo Workflow
The most effective way to hunt for bugs is to feed the files discovered by Puppeteer directly into LinkFinder and SecretFinder.

[Puppeteer] -> Finds and downloads all the active .js script files loaded by the app.
     │
     ├──> [LinkFinder]   -> Scans those scripts to uncover 100% of the hidden API routes.
     └──> [SecretFinder] -> Scans those scripts to find leaked AWS keys or API secrets.

## How to Automate the Combo
Instead of abandoning those tools, you can use Puppeteer to automatically download all Javascript files on a page and pass them to LinkFinder/SecretFinder like this: [4] 

import puppeteer from 'puppeteer';import { exec } from 'child_process';import fs from 'fs';
const browser = await puppeteer.launch({ executablePath: '/usr/bin/chromium-browser', args: ['--no-sandbox'] });const page = await browser.newPage();
// Catch every .js script file the website loads
page.on('response', async (response) => {
  const url = response.url();
  if (url.endsWith('.js')) {
    console.log(`[Found JS File]: ${url}`);
    
    // Download the JS file contents and save it locally
    try {
      const text = await response.text();
      fs.writeFileSync('target_script.js', text);
      
      // Immediately pipe it into SecretFinder/LinkFinder to hunt for bugs!
      exec('python3 SecretFinder.py -i target_script.js -o cli', (err, stdout) => {
        if (stdout) console.log(stdout);
      });
    } catch (e) { /* Handle tracking errors */ }
  }
});
await page.goto('https://target.com');await browser.close();

